WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.718
Someone asks for the content of your site in a spreadsheet.

00:00:04.718 --> 00:00:08.894
In this video, we design a data pipeline that reads the blog posts,

00:00:08.894 --> 00:00:13.070
writes them to a CSV file, and saves the file in the media library.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.034
A data pipeline is a chain of steps.

00:00:20.034 --> 00:00:23.974
A source step reads rows from a data source, such as the content

00:00:23.974 --> 00:00:27.970
items of a content type, the users of the site, or a saved query.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.027
Transform steps change the rows:

00:00:31.027 --> 00:00:33.759
they filter, sort, group and join them.

00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:39.830
This pipeline needs none, because the source itself can choose its fields and filter its rows.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.039
A file step writes the rows to a CSV file, an Excel workbook,

00:00:45.039 --> 00:00:46.703
JSON or JSON Lines.

00:00:46.703 --> 00:00:49.897
A destination step then delivers the file.

00:00:49.897 --> 00:00:54.090
Here, it saves the file in a folder of the media library.

00:00:58.301 --> 00:01:03.641
With the Data Pipelines feature enabled, the Tools menu has a Data Pipelines entry.

00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:08.561
Pipelines lists the pipelines of the site, and Run History lists their runs.

00:01:08.561 --> 00:01:10.181
Let's open Pipelines.

00:01:10.693 --> 00:01:12.332
The list is empty.

00:01:12.332 --> 00:01:18.273
Let's select Add Pipeline, type Blog posts export as the name, and select Create.

00:01:18.723 --> 00:01:20.262
The designer opens.

00:01:20.262 --> 00:01:26.050
The toolbox on the left groups the steps as Sources, Transforms, Files and Destinations.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:30.421
The canvas holds the pipeline, and the panel on the right has the

00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:32.883
Pipeline, Issues and History tabs.

00:01:33.341 --> 00:01:38.312
The Pipeline tab also holds a description, which the list of pipelines shows.

00:01:38.312 --> 00:01:40.828
Let's write what this pipeline does.

00:01:40.828 --> 00:01:45.561
The designer saves a draft as you work, and the toolbar says Draft saved.

00:01:49.861 --> 00:01:52.358
Every pipeline starts with a source.

00:01:52.358 --> 00:01:54.558
Let's select Add a data source.

00:01:54.558 --> 00:01:58.720
The dialog lists the data sets you may read, and its left column

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.742
filters them by data source:

00:02:00.742 --> 00:02:03.061
Content items, Queries and Users.

00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:05.829
Each content type is a data set.

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:09.417
Let's type blog in the Filter box, and add Blog Post.

00:02:09.886 --> 00:02:14.374
The Read a data source step appears on the canvas, and its settings

00:02:14.374 --> 00:02:16.219
open in the panel below.

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:19.416
The data set is Blog Post, from Content items.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.617
Fields to read chooses the columns.

00:02:22.617 --> 00:02:26.155
Let's keep Title, Subtitle, Author and Created.

00:02:26.155 --> 00:02:29.960
With no field selected, the step reads every field.

00:02:30.435 --> 00:02:32.950
Filters keep only the rows that match.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.494
Let's select Add a filter, and set Published equals Yes.

00:02:36.494 --> 00:02:39.295
Posts that are drafts stay out of the file.

00:02:39.748 --> 00:02:43.763
In the Data tab, Preview runs the pipeline up to this step on

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.381
a sample of the rows.

00:02:45.381 --> 00:02:48.558
It shows six rows, one for each published post.

00:02:52.807 --> 00:02:55.932
Select the Rows port at the right edge of the step.

00:02:55.932 --> 00:02:58.508
A list offers the steps that accept rows.

00:02:58.508 --> 00:03:00.317
Let's choose Create a file.

00:03:00.798 --> 00:03:04.175
The Format is CSV, comma separated values, by default.

00:03:04.175 --> 00:03:08.847
The File name is written without its extension, so let's type blog-posts, and

00:03:08.847 --> 00:03:11.548
the step adds the extension of the format.

00:03:12.115 --> 00:03:14.535
Start with a row of column names is on.

00:03:14.535 --> 00:03:18.406
Let's also turn on Name the columns with the labels of the fields,

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:22.815
so the first row uses labels such as Title and Created, not technical names.

00:03:23.388 --> 00:03:26.304
A comma separates the values by default.

00:03:26.304 --> 00:03:30.488
For tab-separated values, type tab in the CSV delimiter box.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:34.458
The Data tab previews the file this step creates:

00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:37.775
its name, its type, its size and its six rows.

00:03:37.775 --> 00:03:39.880
A preview delivers nothing.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:48.023
Select the Files port of the file step, and the list offers the

00:03:48.023 --> 00:03:49.694
steps that accept files.

00:03:49.694 --> 00:03:52.200
Let's choose Save to the media library.

00:03:52.691 --> 00:03:56.647
Folder is the folder of the media library that receives the file.

00:03:56.647 --> 00:03:58.039
Let's type exports.

00:03:58.039 --> 00:04:00.491
Left empty, the file goes to the root.

00:04:00.983 --> 00:04:03.799
The warning is about other formats.

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:09.431
By default, the media library accepts CSV files and Excel workbooks, but not

00:04:09.431 --> 00:04:11.903
JSON, JSON Lines or zip files.

00:04:12.368 --> 00:04:17.582
Replace a file with the same name is on, so each run overwrites the previous file.

00:04:17.582 --> 00:04:21.848
Without it, the file is saved under a name with a number appended.

00:04:22.308 --> 00:04:25.654
The Data tab shows what the step would receive:

00:04:25.654 --> 00:04:26.601
the file.

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:32.218
Destinations are never executed by a preview, so the media library stays untouched.

00:04:36.518 --> 00:04:40.098
The Issues tab lists what must be fixed before the pipeline can be

00:04:40.098 --> 00:04:42.186
published, and what deserves a look.

00:04:42.186 --> 00:04:43.528
It reports no issues.

00:04:43.997 --> 00:04:47.604
A run executes the published version, never the draft.

00:04:47.604 --> 00:04:49.227
Let's select Publish.

00:04:49.227 --> 00:04:53.617
A message confirms it, and the toolbar shows the published version.

00:04:57.817 --> 00:05:02.111
A published pipeline runs with the access of the user who published it.

00:05:02.111 --> 00:05:05.737
It reads and writes only what that user may read and write.

00:05:06.487 --> 00:05:13.174
That is why the Manage data pipelines permission, which covers designing and publishing, is security critical.

00:05:13.174 --> 00:05:19.227
Run data pipelines allows running them, and View data pipelines and their runs allows viewing them.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:24.807
Let's select Run.

00:05:24.807 --> 00:05:28.239
The run is queued, and it executes in the background.

00:05:28.239 --> 00:05:30.740
The History tab opens and follows it.

00:05:31.209 --> 00:05:34.237
When the run ends, its status is Succeeded.

00:05:34.237 --> 00:05:39.058
The read step shows six rows, and the last two steps show one file each.

00:05:39.058 --> 00:05:42.149
Deliveries records where the file was saved.

00:05:42.614 --> 00:05:45.081
Select Open the run for the full page.

00:05:45.081 --> 00:05:49.119
It lists the status of each step, what was delivered, and the log.

00:05:49.119 --> 00:05:52.764
The Run History link there lists the runs of this pipeline.

00:05:57.028 --> 00:06:00.886
Let's open Media, Library, and select the exports folder.

00:06:00.886 --> 00:06:04.008
The CSV file is here, with the name we typed.

00:06:04.494 --> 00:06:06.544
Each run replaces the file.

00:06:06.544 --> 00:06:11.454
Open it in a spreadsheet application, and each published post is one row.

00:06:13.454 --> 00:06:17.738
A source, a file and a destination are enough to export content in

00:06:17.738 --> 00:06:19.642
a format other tools read.

00:06:19.642 --> 00:06:25.057
Separate videos cover running pipelines on a schedule, and delivering files by email.

00:06:25.057 --> 00:06:26.604
Thanks for watching.

